# DESIGN — <Project Name>

House design contract scaffolded from the webapp-design skill
(`~/.agents/skills/webapp-design/`). House defaults are the "unless overridden" baseline;
fill every `DECIDE:` field with the real per-product decision.

## Brand (DECIDE each)

- Palette: DECIDE: primary / accent / surface / text tokens (avoid AI-purple defaults)
- Typography: DECIDE: type scale + fonts (UI + display)
- Tone: DECIDE: voice (professional / warm / playful) — applies to copy
- Audience: DECIDE: who this product is for
- Motion: DECIDE: default easing + duration (default ease-out; `:active` scale(0.97))

## Taste guardrails (house — do not weaken)

- No em-dashes / en-dashes in UI copy (use " - "). No hero-label cliches, scroll cues, decorative status dots.
- No lorem ipsum — real data or honest placeholders.
- Semantic HTML, accessible-first. No AI-purple gradients/glow as default.
- CI gate: `npx impeccable detect` over generated HTML when a Node toolchain exists.

## Stack

Performance-first language choice, per project (see house rule): default to **Go** for
bootstrap / internal / all-else-equal; **Rust** when externally-facing performance or safety
guarantees are load-bearing. Both are single-binary hypermedia + SQLite (WAL), no-API.

- **Go**: stdlib `http.ServeMux` (Go 1.22+ method routing) + a-h/templ + modernc.org/sqlite +
  embedded htmx 2.x. No node; the only build step is `templ generate`.
- **Rust (Axum) + HTMX + SQLite (sqlx)**: when the compiler's guarantees are the point.
- **Alpine.js 3.x** (embedded, pinned like htmx) owns browser-local UI state: dropdowns,
  modals, tabs, accordions, client-side filtering. htmx owns server round-trips. ~15KB, no
  build step.
- Radix UI + TS islands only where genuinely complex interactivity needs them. No-API: JSON only for mobile/external.

## Engineering patterns

### Templates (Go)

- a-h/templ (default) — `.templ` files compile to typed Go functions (`templ generate`, no
  node, <500ms); wrong prop type is a build error, not a 500.
- `templ.Handler(component, templ.WithStreaming())` for HTMX partials; branch on the
  `HX-Request` header to return fragment vs full page from the same handler.
- Fallback: stdlib `html/template` only for zero-codegen minimal apps (loses composition +
  type checking).
- Pass Go data into Alpine state via `x-data={ templ.JSONString(data) }` (attribute-escaped
  valid JSON); `templ.JSONScript("id", data)` passes JSON in a script element and supports
  CSP nonces (`templ.CSPNonceFromContext`).
- `templ.OnceHandle` renders a global script (Alpine store init, plugin registration) once
  per response. Do not drive `x-*` attributes from templ `script` components (they compile to
  `__templ_*` functions) - pass data via attributes, behavior via `Alpine.data()` factories.

### SQLite driver + pooling (Go)

- Driver: **modernc.org/sqlite v1.55** (pure-Go transpilation, `CGO_ENABLED=0` static binary;
  same query planner/WAL semantics as CGO mattn; perf delta unobservable at single-VPS scale).
- DSN pragmas (pocketbase/headscale/gitea-proven), `busy_timeout` FIRST:
  `_pragma=busy_timeout(10000)&_pragma=journal_mode(WAL)&_pragma=synchronous(NORMAL)&_pragma=foreign_keys(ON)&_txlock=immediate`
- Pool: **`SetMaxOpenConns(1)`** — SQLite has exactly one writer; multiple pooled connections
  interleaving WAL checkpoints causes `SQLITE_NOTADB`/WAL-desync corruption
  (charmbracelet/crush). Keep one transaction per request (single-conn pools deadlock on nested tx).
- Access layer: sqlc v1.31.1 (typed codegen) or sqlx v1.4.0; no ORMs. Migrations: goose v3.27.2.
- WAL-reset corruption bug fixed in SQLite 3.51.3 (2026-03-13) — pin ≥ that in driver + litestream.

### Replication / backup (Litestream)

- Litestream v0.5.15 sidecar (or `replicate -exec` supervisor) streams WAL pages as LTX files;
  RPO ≈ sync-interval (default 1s; raise to `1m` for low-write apps), restore via
  `litestream restore [-timestamp ...]`. No `verify` subcommand — verify via
  `restore -integrity-check full` + `PRAGMA integrity_check`.
- OCI Object Storage target: explicit `endpoint: https://<ns>.compat.objectstorage.<region>.oraclecloud.com`,
  `region: us-east-1` (never your OCI region id), Customer Secret Keys (not API keys),
  DELETE-only lifecycle rules (IA/Archive breaks restore), separate bucket from restic.
- Config: `snapshot.interval: 1h` (default 24h restores too slow), `snapshot.retention: 168h`,
  `l0-retention: 15m`, `heartbeat-url` dead-man's switch. One replica destination per DB (v0.5 rule).
- Layering: Litestream (RPO ~1s) + nightly `VACUUM INTO` snapshot to object storage. Never
  `cp` a live WAL db. Long-lived read txs stall checkpoints → set `truncate-page-n: 0` +
  alert on `-wal` size.

### HTMX patterns (shared)

- `HX-Request` header distinguishes full page vs fragment from the same handler.
- `hx-target` extended selectors (closest / next / find / this); `hx-swap` table (innerHTML,
  outerHTML, beforeend, afterbegin, delete, none).
- Error responses are swapable HTML fragments; use `hx-status:422` / `hx-status:500` targeting.
- `hx-indicator` for loading states (`.htmx-request` toggled automatically).
- `hx-swap-oob` for multi-element updates; SSE via `hx-trigger="sse:..."` + `EventSource`.
- Go: `go:embed htmx.min.js` (pin htmx 2.0.9 GitHub release tag — npm `latest` dist-tag is broken).
  Helper: `angelofallars/htmx-go` optional; most apps just read `HX-Request`.

### Alpine.js patterns (shared)

- **Ownership split**: htmx owns network + DOM swapping; Alpine owns local UI state and
  coordination. The glue is events, not direct API calls.
- **Debuggability bias**: prefer an htmx round-trip over Alpine client state for anything
  correctness-related (filters, form progress, flags) - server-owned state is what an agent
  can assert from HTML + logs. Alpine for pure-UI state only.
- Loading flags: listen `htmx:beforeRequest` / `htmx:afterRequest` on `window`, scope the
  reaction with `$el.contains($event.target)` so nested requests don't leak across components.
- Alpine -> htmx: `$dispatch` a custom event that `hx-trigger` listens for, or call
  `htmx.ajax('GET', url, '#target')` directly from `@click`.
- htmx -> Alpine: `@htmx:after-request` on the swapped fragment (e.g. close a modal on
  submit); or a global `htmx:afterSwap` listener updating an `Alpine.store`.
- An htmx swap destroys Alpine state in the replaced region - write components idempotent:
  state via `Alpine.data()` factories, listeners on the element, no global side effects.
  Alpine's mutation observer auto-initializes swapped-in `x-*` content; htmx attributes in
  Alpine-rendered DOM need `x-init="$nextTick(() => htmx.process($el))"`.
- Register reusable components with `Alpine.data()` (one init cost, shared prototype); keep
  large datasets out of `x-data`; `$watch` sparingly, `x-effect` for reactive effects.
- Plugins: focus (`x-trap` for accessible modals; `.inert` / `.noscroll`), collapse (height
  animation), intersect (lazy init). Animate `opacity`/`transform` only; respect
  `prefers-reduced-motion`. Prefer `x-show` over `x-if` for frequently toggled elements.
- Pin + embed Alpine like htmx (`go:embed` the pinned release build); Alpine stores/plugins
  live under `static/`.

### Project structure (Go)

```
cmd/app/main.go       # entry: mux, middleware, sql pools
internal/routes/      # page handlers (full page) + fragment handlers (no layout)
internal/db/          # sqlc/sqlx queries, pools (single-conn writer)
internal/domain/      # models + business logic
internal/views/       # templ sources (generated .go alongside)
static/               # go:embed htmx.min.js + alpine.min.js + assets
migrations/           # goose sql migrations
```

Middleware order: security headers (CSP) -> recoverer -> request logging -> rate limit -> CSRF.

### Templates (Rust)

- askama (default, compile-time typed, Jinja-like) or maud (HTML macros / component streaming).
- No runtime template engine by default. Auto-escape by default; raw/safe only for trusted content.
- No stringly-typed template names — the type system is the namespace (kills the Go
  dynamic-name / ParseGlob-collision failure class). Precompilation is inherent (compiled in).

### Project structure (Axum)

```
src/main.rs            # entry: router, state, middleware chain
src/routes/mod.rs      # Router composition
src/routes/pages.rs    # full-page handlers
src/routes/fragments.rs# HTMX fragment handlers (no layout)
src/routes/api.rs      # JSON API (mobile/external only)
src/state.rs           # AppState: pool, templates, broadcast channels
src/db.rs              # sqlx pool / rusqlite
src/domain/            # models + business logic
templates/             # askama sources
static/                # embedded via rust-embed (unless CDN)
```

Middleware order (tower): CORS -> trace -> recovery -> security headers (CSP) -> rate limit -> CSRF.

### Forms

- Multi-step = one HTMX request per step (`hx-target` + `hx-swap="outerHTML"`).
- Validation errors returned as HTML fragments, not JSON (`HX-Retarget` / `hx-status:422`);
  Go: validate in the handler and re-render the form fragment; Rust: `validator` crate + axum extractors.
- Flash messages via `HX-Trigger` + an OOB toast element.
- Alpine for instant client-side feedback (required/format checks, disable-on-submit) as a
  complement; server validation stays authoritative and returns the rendered fragments.

### Performance

- Compile-time templates (templ/askama/maud — zero runtime parsing); render into a buffer, no
  string concatenation in loops; embedded assets (`go:embed` / `rust-embed`) — single-binary deploy.
- Go image ~15–25MB stripped with modernc; `-trimpath -ldflags="-s -w" -tags netgo,osusergo`.
- SQLite: `synchronous=NORMAL` in WAL (one fsync per checkpoint, not per commit).
- Alpine: `x-show` over `x-if` for frequently toggled elements; `Alpine.data()` factories over
  inline `x-data` when many instances share a shape; animate `opacity`/`transform` only
  (`x-collapse` for accordions); `x-init` + `$nextTick` for expensive setup.

### Security

- CSRF: same-origin HTMX + `HX-CSRF-Token` header check via middleware (Go: hand-rolled /
  gorilla-csrf; Rust: axum-csrf); 403 on mismatch.
- CSP: `default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'`, `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`,
  `X-Frame-Options: DENY`. Tighten script-src once inline scripts are removed. `connect-src
  'self'` (or the `default-src` fallback) permits `hx-*` requests.
- Alpine: standard build needs `script-src 'unsafe-eval'` (it evaluates `x-*` expressions via
  `new Function`). Internal apps add it. The CSP build (`@alpinejs/csp`) runs without
  `unsafe-eval` under `script-src 'nonce-*' 'strict-dynamic'` but limits expressions to named
  properties/methods (`Alpine.data()` factories; no `x-model`, no inline expressions).
  PCI-DSS 4.0 (2025-04-01+) requires strict CSP on payment pages - write components against
  `Alpine.data()` factories from day one so the CSP migration stays cheap.
- Sanitization: auto-escape by default (templ/askama/maud auto-escape; html/template does too);
  raw/safe only for trusted content; validate path/query params; escape attribute context for
  user content placed in `hx-*` / `x-*` attributes.
- SQLite ≥ 3.51.3 in app + litestream (WAL-reset corruption bug, fixed 2026-03-13).

### Testing

- Go: `httptest` + `net/http/httptest.ResponseRecorder`; assert both full-page and
  `HX-Request` fragment responses (read `r.Header.Get("HX-Request")`).
- Rust: `tower::ServiceExt::oneshot` + `axum::body::to_bytes`; assert both full-page and
  `HX-Request` fragment responses.
- Snapshot tests over rendered HTML fragments (Go: `go-cmp`/golden files; Rust: `insta`).
- E2E: Playwright for HTMX interactions (navigate, click, fill, wait for swapped selectors).
- E2E: assert Alpine state across swaps - state reset on swap is intended, so assert the fresh
  baseline; any server-visible state change must be assertable from the returned HTML.

### Docker

- Go static binary: `CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -trimpath -ldflags="-s -w -extldflags '-static'"`
  `-tags netgo,osusergo` in a `golang:1.x-alpine` builder; runtime `cgr.dev/chainguard/static`
  (distroless, no shell) or `alpine:3.x` nonroot; optionally `ko` (builds distroless from source).
- Rust static binary: `rustup target add x86_64-unknown-linux-musl`, or `rust:*-alpine` builder
  with a `distroless/static` runtime. Verify `file <bin>` reports "statically linked".
- `.dockerignore` can silently exclude the binary — check it before debugging `COPY` checksum errors.
- Templates are compiled in: a production template change requires a rebuild
  (dev watchers are dev-only; in prod rebuild the image or `docker cp` the new binary + restart).
- Litestream: sidecar container sharing the named volume with the app (both Linux-host),
  `litestream/litestream:0.5-scratch`, or supervisor pattern `litestream replicate -exec "app"`;
  restore-on-start guarded by `if [ ! -f "$DB_PATH" ]` + `-if-replica-exists`; delete stale
  `-wal`/`-shm` before restore.

### Cloudflare Workers escalation (when the house default isn't enough)

Escalate from single-binary self-host when: global edge distribution, zero-ops serverless,
per-tenant isolation at scale, real-time/coordinated state, or free-tier prototyping is the
driving constraint. The frontend (HTMX + Radix islands + PWA) is unchanged.

- **D1** (default Cloudflare relational layer, SQLite semantics): read-heavy workloads, free
  global read replicas, per-tenant database sharding (10 GB/DB, 100 cols, 2 MB rows, 100 KB SQL,
  1000 queries/invocation paid; 50/invocation free), HTTP API for non-Worker tooling.
- **Durable Objects (SQLite-backed)**: stateful per-entity compute + storage colocated —
  single-threaded, globally unique, strictly serializable. Use for WebSockets, presence,
  coordination, per-user/per-entity state (~1,000 req/s soft limit per object; 10 GB/object).
- **R2** for PWA/assets/media (no egress charges); **KV** for config/routing metadata only.
- Same SQL limits apply in both (2 MB rows, 100 cols, 100 KB statements); writes are row-billed
  (paid: first 50M writes/mo included, then $1/M; reads first 25B/mo, then $0.001/M).
- Do NOT escalate when: single-region/small app, heavy writes, large/complex SQL, or when
  in-process sqlite latency is the point. Self-hosted sqlite is faster than any remote DB.
